.TH HI 1 "2026-08-25" "say-hi" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
hi \- your shell config, on every host you say hi to
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hi
.RI [ ssh\-options ]
.I target
.RI [ command ...]
.br
.B hi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hi
copies your
.B say-hi
configuration tree (prompt, colors, aliases, vim/nano configs, connect
header) to a target, starts a session there, and removes every trace when
the session ends. It is sshrc, supercharged.
.PP
With no
.IR command ,
.B hi
opens an interactive session on the target: your login shell when hi
styles it (bash, zsh or fish),
otherwise the best of fish > zsh > bash. Both halves come off one
ordering \- fish > zsh > bash > dash > ash > sh \- so a
target with no bash at all falls back to the best of the rest of it:
fish > zsh > dash > ash > sh, with hi's aliases and colored
prompt but no header. With a trailing
.IR command ,
it runs that command on the target and exits, like
.BR ssh (1)
does.
.PP
The payload is base64\-armored through the target's login shell for ssh
targets, unpacked into a private
.BR mktemp (1)
directory, and removed by cleanup traps on exit \- including on an abrupt
disconnect. If the target has a permanent say-hi (installed with
.BR install.sh ,
anywhere \- the location is read from that target's login rc files, and failing
that from the standard install prefixes), that copy is used in place and left
alone.
.SH TARGET RESOLUTION
.I target
is tried against each backend, in order; the first match wins:
.TP
.B 1. ssh host
a literal
.B Host
entry in
.B ~/.ssh/config
(wildcard entries are not matched).
.TP
.B 2. docker, then podman container
a running container, by name or ID.
.TP
.B 3. nomad allocation
a running allocation, by ID or ID prefix.
.TP
.B 4. kubernetes pod
a running pod in the current
.BR kubectl (1)
context and namespace, or
.I namespace:pod
and
.I context:namespace:pod
for one elsewhere \- the prefixes become
.B kubectl \-\-context
and
.BR "\-\-namespace" .
Completion spells a pod outside the current namespace the same way.
.PP
The last two can hold more than one thing to land in, and a
.B /
suffix says which:
.I alloc/task
becomes
.B nomad alloc exec \-task
and
.I pod/container
becomes
.BR "kubectl exec \-c" .
Without it the two differ:
.BR nomad (1)
refuses a multi-task exec outright, while
.BR kubectl (1)
falls back to the pod's first container and warns. Completion offers the
pairs for any allocation or pod that has more than one.
.PP
A name matching none of these is handed to
.BR ssh (1)
anyway, so unlisted hosts still work.
.PP
.BR \-\-ssh ,
.BR \-\-docker ,
.BR \-\-podman ,
.BR \-\-nomad
or
.B \-\-kube
before
.I target
names the arm outright and skips every probe above it \- the fix for a
container that shadows an unrelated ssh host of the same name, or the other
way around.
.SH NO TARGET
.B hi
with no
.I target
and no
.I ssh\-options
offers the list instead of failing \- the same rows the shell completions read,
so the offer is backend\-tagged, ranked by what you connected to recently, and
served out of the
.B $_HI_TARGETS_TTL
cache a
.B TAB
may already have warmed. Picking one connects to it.
.PP
.BR fzf (1)
is used when it is on the client, then
.BR sk (1);
with neither, a numbered
.B select
menu, so nothing has to be installed for this to work. The picker runs on the
client only and never reaches a target.
.PP
Both ends of a terminal are required, so
.B hi
in a script or a CI job goes on printing the usage message from
.BR ssh (1)
and failing rather than waiting on a menu nobody can answer. An
.I ssh\-option
with no target does the same: an option without a host is an error for
.BR ssh (1)
to report, not a target to guess at \- which is what keeps
.B hi \-V
behaving as
.B ssh \-V
does.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-h\fR, \fB\-\-help
print a usage summary \- the synopsis, the target resolution order, and hi's
own options \- and exit. Everything here in short form; this page is the long
one. Caught before the pass\-through below, so it never reaches
.BR ssh (1).
.TP
.B \-\-version
print the version and exit: the version stamped in at packaging time, or
.B git describe
output in a checkout.
.TP
.B \-\-ssh
force the ssh arm for
.IR target ,
skipping every backend probe in
.B TARGET RESOLUTION
above \- ssh's own error speaks if it is not there.
.TP
.B \-\-docker
force the docker arm for
.IR target .
.TP
.B \-\-podman
force the podman arm for
.IR target .
.TP
.B \-\-nomad
force the nomad arm for
.IR target .
.TP
.B \-\-kube
force the kube arm for
.IR target .
.TP
.B \-\-plain
skip the payload entirely and hand over a bare shell on whichever arm
.I target
resolves to \- no copy, no aliases, no header. Needs nothing from the target
beyond a shell (and, for an ssh target,
.BR sshd (8))
\- not even
.BR tar (1)
or
.BR base64 (1),
and no writable
.B /tmp
or
.B $HOME
either.
.PP
The following act on this machine instead of connecting, and each needs a part
of the tree that is not always present. All of them want
.BR scripts/ ,
which a package\-manager install ships but a hi session does not \- so inside a
session each says so and stops.
.B \-\-test
and
.B \-\-update
want
.B tests/
and
.B .git
on top of that, and a package carries neither.
.B \-\-packages\-preview
is the one exception to all of it: it falls back to the check itself, out of the
shipped
.BR common/header.sh ,
rather than refusing.
.TP
.B \-\-doctor \fR[\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-ssh\fR|\fB\-\-docker\fR|\fB\-\-podman\fR|\fB\-\-nomad\fR|\fB\-\-kube\fR] [\fItarget\fR]
print a read\-only pre\-flight report instead of connecting: the local tree,
the config overlay, every backend probed and timed, and \- with a target \-
which backend the name resolves to plus an ssh reachability check. A backend
flag reports the same forced arm a real connect with it would take, skipping
the probe chain. With
.BR \-\-json ,
the same rows as one JSON document on stdout \- what a bug report should
carry \- with the finding count as the exit status either way.
.TP
.B \-\-install\fR, \fB\-\-uninstall
install or repair say-hi's lines in your shell rc files, or take them back
out again.
.TP
.B \-\-configure
revisit the settings only, leaving the rc wiring alone: features, header
details, package\-check depth, width, the prompt, and an advanced section
one Enter skips. Every question shows its current value and previews what it
decides; nothing is written until the last one. Opens with a choice of preset
(\fBeverything\fR, \fBbalanced\fR, \fBminimal\fR) as a starting point;
.B \-\-preset <name>
after it applies one without asking.
.TP
.B \-\-check\-configs
re\-run just the syntax validation, without the rest of the install: the
shell rc files, and the overlay's own shell files (\fBaliases.sh\fR under both
\fBsh\fR and \fBfish\fR, since a target sources it in whichever it has).
.TP
.B \-\-overlay\-init
make the config overlay at
.B ~/.config/say-hi/
a git repo, in place, so it carries history.
.TP
.B \-\-update
.B git pull
in the say-hi checkout.
Needs one: an install from a package has no
.B .git
to pull into, and says so rather than running.
.TP
.B \-\-color\-preview
what every ssh host and your user resolve to, in their actual colors.
.TP
.B \-\-packages\-preview
the package\-priority legend, as the header prints it.
.TP
.B \-\-test
run the test suite.
Needs the full checkout: the packages do not ship
.BR tests/ ,
and there this says so rather than running.
.PP
Everything else is passed through to
.BR ssh (1)
unchanged \-
.BR \-p ,
.BR \-i ,
.BR \-J ,
.BR \-o ,
and the rest behave exactly as they do there. Only the first
non\-option word is taken as the target; everything after it is the
remote command.
.SH COMPLETION
.B hi
completes in bash, zsh and fish. All three read one list \-
.B common/targets.sh
\- so the three can not drift apart, and it is the only one fish can run.
.TP
.B hi <TAB>
every target: the
.B Host
entries in
.B ~/.ssh/config
plus every running container, allocation and pod, each tagged with the
backend it came from. The list is reused for
.B $_HI_TARGETS_TTL
seconds and each backend gets
.B $_HI_PROBE_TIMEOUT
to answer, because this runs on every keypress.
.TP
.B hi \-\-<TAB>
hi's own options, answered without probing any backend at all. The list is
filtered to what this install can actually run: a session is offered none of
the flags that need
.BR scripts/ ,
and a package\-manager install neither of the two that need
.B tests/
or
.BR .git .
.SH CONFIGURATION
Configuration lives outside the tree, in
.B ~/.config/say-hi/
.RB ( settings.sh ,
.BR colors ,
.BR packages ,
additive
.BR aliases.sh ,
and per-shell
.BR bash.sh ,
.B zsh.zsh
and
.BR config.fish ),
written by
.B hi \-\-configure
and carried along to every target. Every feature is an environment
variable toggle \- header, prompt, git status, editors, clipboard \- each on
by default and tabulated in
.IR docs/SETTINGS.md .
The tree itself can be
root\-owned (a package\-manager install): nothing hi does writes into it.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /usr/share/say-hi
the tree, for package\-manager installs (a git checkout at
.B ~/say-hi
works identically).
.TP
.B ~/.config/say-hi/settings.sh
feature toggles, written by
.BR "hi \-\-configure" .
.TP
.B ~/.config/say-hi/colors, ~/.config/say-hi/packages
per\-user overrides of the tree's defaults.
.TP
.B ~/.config/say-hi/aliases.sh
your own aliases, sourced after the shipped set so yours win.
.TP
.B ~/.config/say-hi/bash.sh, ~/.config/say-hi/zsh.zsh, ~/.config/say-hi/config.fish
your own per\-shell preferences, sourced after hi's own so yours win.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B _HI_HOME
the parent of the
.B say-hi
directory; set by
.B /etc/profile.d/say-hi.sh
for packaged installs.
.TP
.B _HI_SHELL_PREFERENCE
ordered list of session shells to try
.RB ( login
means your login shell); default
.BR login ,
then the shells hi styles in preference order:
.BR "fish zsh bash" .
.TP
.B _HI_DISABLE_HEADER, _HI_DISABLE_PROMPT, ...
per\-feature opt\-outs; see
.I docs/SETTINGS.md
for the full table.
.TP
.B _HI_COLORS, _HI_PACKAGES, _HI_VIMRC, _HI_NANORC
where each of those four config files is read from. Unset, each resolves to
the copy in
.B $_HI_CONFIG_DIR
if you have made one and to the tree's otherwise; exported, it points that
one file elsewhere and leaves the rest of the overlay where it is.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B hi prod-db
interactive session on the ssh host
.BR prod\-db ,
with your prompt, colors and aliases.
.TP
.B hi -J bastion internal 'df -h'
run a command on
.B internal
through a jump host.
.TP
.B hi 8f41c2
session inside a running container or allocation with that ID.
.TP
.B hi
no target: pick one from the list \- see
.B NO TARGET
above.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ssh (1)
.PP
Full documentation \- the toggle tables in
.IR docs/SETTINGS.md ,
the testing story in
.IR docs/TESTING.md ,
and the README over both:
.I https://github.com/ivylikethevine/say-hi
